          id: '101_1_15_2',
          question:
            'Examine the social and cultural life of the Early Vedic Age.',
          answer:
            'INTRODUCTION\nThe Early Vedic Age (c. 1500–1000 BCE) marks the arrival of the Aryans in the Indian subcontinent and the composition of the Rigveda—the oldest known literary text in the world. The Aryans initially settled in the region of Sapta-Sindhu (seven rivers of the Punjab) before gradually moving eastward. Their society and culture, as reflected in the Rigveda, formed the foundation of Hindu civilization.\n\nSOCIAL ORGANIZATION\nEarly Vedic society was organized around kinship units: the family (kula), clan (grama), tribal unit (vish), and tribe (jana). Society was relatively fluid; varna divisions existed but were not rigidly hereditary. The four varnas—Brahmin (priests), Kshatriya (warriors), Vaishya (commoners), and Shudra (servants)—were based on occupation rather than birth. Family was patriarchal, though women enjoyed considerable freedom.\n\nPOSITION OF WOMEN\nWomen in the Early Vedic Age held a respected place in society. They participated in religious ceremonies (yajnas), could study the Vedas, and some composed hymns—Gargi and Maitreyi being the most celebrated scholars. Child marriage was unknown, and widow remarriage was permitted through the niyoga system. Women could attend public assemblies (Sabha and Samiti), indicating social participation.\n\nECONOMIC LIFE\nThe Early Vedic economy was primarily pastoral. Cattle was the chief measure of wealth; the word for "war" (gavishti) literally means "desire for cattle." Agriculture was secondary. The Aryans produced chariots, pottery, and woven cloth. Trade was by barter, though the term nishka (gold ornament) appears as a unit of value. The economy was largely self-sufficient at the village level.\n\nRELIGION\nVedic religion centered on nature worship. The Aryans worshipped personified natural forces: Indra (thunder and war), Agni (fire), Varuna (cosmic order), Surya (sun), Usha (dawn), and Soma (the sacred plant). Worship was through yajnas (fire sacrifices) accompanied by Vedic hymns. Religion was relatively simple—without temples or idol worship—and focused on maintaining cosmic order (Rita).\n\nLITERATURE AND KNOWLEDGE\nThe Rigveda, with 1,028 hymns in 10 Mandalas, is the primary literary achievement of this age. The hymns reveal deep philosophical thinking about creation, cosmic order, and the nature of gods and humanity. The Rigveda contains the famous Nasadiya Sukta (Hymn of Creation) questioning the origin of the universe—one of the earliest recorded philosophical speculations.\n\nPOLITICAL ORGANIZATION\nEarly Vedic polity was tribal. The tribe was led by a Raja (chief), assisted by a Purohita (priest) and Senani (military commander). Two assemblies—Sabha (council of elders) and Samiti (general tribal assembly)—checked the chief\'s power. The position of Raja was not fully hereditary; tribal consent was important. Warfare was common, mainly to capture cattle.\n\nCONCLUSION\nThe Early Vedic Age was a formative period in Indian history. A relatively egalitarian society with nature worship, rich oral literature, and active female participation gradually evolved into the more complex, ritualistic civilization of the Later Vedic period. The intellectual and spiritual seeds planted in this age—in the form of the Vedas—continued to nourish Indian civilization for millennia.',
