          id: '101_5_15_1',
          question: 'Examine the impact of Western education on Indian society.',
          answer:
            'INTRODUCTION\nThe introduction of Western education under British colonial rule was one of the most transformative developments in modern Indian history. Beginning with missionary schools in the late 18th century and formalized by Macaulay\'s Minute (1835) and Wood\'s Dispatch (1854), Western education created a new English-educated Indian middle class that both collaborated with and challenged colonial rule, and eventually led India\'s independence movement.\n\nPOLICY EVOLUTION\nInitially, the East India Company avoided interfering in Indian education. The Charter Act of 1813 provided funds for Indian education but left the language question unresolved, creating the "Orientalist vs. Anglicist" debate. The Orientalists (H.H. Wilson, H.T. Prinsep) argued for promoting Sanskrit and Persian; the Anglicists (Macaulay, Alexander Duff) argued for English. Macaulay\'s Minute (1835) resolved the debate in favor of English, creating the framework for colonial education.\n\nWOOD\'S DISPATCH (1854)\nSir Charles Wood\'s Dispatch of 1854 provided the structural framework for Indian education: a hierarchy from primary (vernacular medium) through secondary to university (English medium); grants-in-aid to private schools; establishment of universities (Calcutta, Bombay, Madras established 1857); and attention to female education. This created India\'s modern education system.\n\nCREATION OF MIDDLE CLASS\nWestern education\'s most significant social impact was the creation of a westernized Indian middle class—educated in English, familiar with Western liberal and democratic ideas, employed in colonial administration and the professions. This class included administrators, lawyers, doctors, journalists, and teachers. It was from this class that India\'s reformers, social activists, and ultimately nationalist leaders emerged.\n\nSPREAD OF MODERN IDEAS\nWestern education brought with it Enlightenment ideas: liberty, equality, rationalism, democracy, nationalism, and human rights. Indians who read John Stuart Mill, Locke, Voltaire, and Rousseau began to apply these concepts to their own situation. The contradiction between colonial rule and liberal ideals was not lost on educated Indians—it became one of the primary motivations for the nationalist movement.\n\nIMPACT ON WOMEN\'S EDUCATION\nWestern education gradually opened opportunities for women. Missionary schools educated girls. Reformers like Jyotirao Phule (who founded India\'s first girls\' school in Pune in 1848), Pandita Ramabai, and Savitribai Phule pioneered female education against social resistance. The Bethune School, Calcutta (1849) and Isabella Thoburn\'s Lucknow Women\'s College (1870) were milestones. Female education eventually empowered women to participate in public life.\n\nIMPACT ON REGIONAL LANGUAGES\nWhile Western education promoted English, the need to reach wider audiences and compete with missionary publications stimulated the modernization of Indian vernacular languages. Bengali, Marathi, Urdu, Tamil, and other languages developed modern prose styles, newspapers, novels, and literary criticism in the 19th century—largely in response to the stimulus of Western literary forms.\n\nNATIONALISM\nThe Indian National Congress (1885) was founded overwhelmingly by Western-educated Indians—lawyers, journalists, teachers. Men like Gopal Krishna Gokhale, Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Surendranath Banerjea, and Bipin Chandra Pal used their Western education to argue for Indian rights in the language of Western liberalism. Gandhi, too, was a product of Western legal education, even as he developed a philosophy of non-violent resistance drawing on Indian tradition.\n\nCRITICISMS\nWestern education had significant drawbacks: it devalued Indian knowledge systems; created an alienated elite disconnected from the masses; made English the language of power and aspiration; neglected technical, agricultural, and vocational education; and served primarily colonial administrative needs. Gandhi criticized it as "denationalization" that produced Indians "English in taste, in opinions, in morals, and in intellect."\n\nCONCLUSION\nThe impact of Western education on India was paradoxical: an instrument of colonial control that also produced the tools of anticolonial resistance. It created social reform movements, nationalist politics, modern literature, and eventually the leaders who ended British rule. Post-independence India inherited both the benefits and the pathologies of this colonial legacy, and debates about the language of instruction and the value of traditional knowledge systems continue to this day.',
